        "id": "s1_regions",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "What the section shows",
        "marks": 0,
        "why": "Grey matter is where the nerve cell bodies sit and white matter is the tracts running past them, so in section the grey lies inside and the white outside.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Putting white matter inside and grey outside. In the CORD the grey is inner; it is the brain that is the other way round.",
          "Drawing the dorsal and ventral horns the same size. The ventral horns are broader."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "In the cord the butterfly of grey matter sits inside; in the brain the grey is outside.",
        "margin_note": "A one-line orientation. The marks are all in the figure.",
        "lines": [
          "In transverse section the spinal cord shows",
          "inner grey matter shaped like a butterfly,",
          "surrounded by outer white matter.",
          "A narrow central canal runs through the",
          "middle of the grey matter."
        ]
